n a  particular group of different outcomes  so it's like the probability of a  positive classification within a  particular group but if you're sampling  groups differently if you're sampling  everyone in one particular group but  only high-risk individuals and another  particular  you're gonna throw off that ratio and  again compromise the utility of this  particular metric when you're trying to  assess fairness so the last subtlety is  that the consequences of your model  matter if you're making a model that is  punitive the consequences of your models  decisions are negative you're handing  out a punishment that I could see making  a case for caring more about false  positives you care about not punishing  innocent people based on the output of  your model similarly if your model is  assistive like it's handing out  something good handing out something  positive I could see you caring more  about false negatives not wasting your  resource or resources on people who  don't need the benefit that you're  handing out I'm not saying this is the  only way to think about punitive and  assistive models like I could see  compelling cases for other arguments  like caring about other outcomes but the  point is that you have to think  carefully about this question like you  have to think about what the  consequences of your model are and that  can inform what kind of errors matter to  you so the final takeaway from kind of  these three points these three  subtleties that I want to stress is that  we can't math our way out of thinking  about fairness like these these are  human questions with ethical  implications and you need some human  judgment to make an appropriate decision  about how you define fairness in your  context and then how do you go about  measuring it I think historically there  was a temptation when machine learning  models first started being used to make  these decisions to say models are just  math and computers so they can't be  unfair and at this point that has been  pretty thoroughly debunked like that's I  think not a common opinion anymore but  there's a temptation to take the same  logic take the same reasoning and just  move it one step farther down the food  chain it's not the models are math so  they must be fair it's I'm gonna add a  little bit of extra math on top of my  model and then it'll be fair but you  can't just rely on the math you still  need a human person applying human  judgments  what makes your model fare so these are  kind of the subtleties of this question  of how you should think about measuring  the fairness of your models output next  I'm going to talk about kind of an  example case study experiment we did at  civis using some real-world data from  one of our consulting engagements to  show how these kind of subtleties and  complications can manifest when you're  doing this with real data that has like  real complicated correlations and lots  of complicated causal pathways within it  so to start off with we're going to work  with a data set from one of our  consulting engagements the features here  are largely demographics and like  socio-economic type indicators and the  outcome is the probability that a person  will sign up for the services of a state  agency and what we're going to look at  is we're going to try to assess racial  bias so we're gonna look at modelled  outcomes for white people versus black  people and to do that we're gonna kind  of make two fake worlds so in one of  these worlds we're going to artificially  balance ground truth so remember that  was the first subtlety that you have to  think about when you're doing this kind  of exercises is ground truth balance  between the different classes that I  care about here we're making a  hypothetical world where ground truth is  totally balanced and we do that by  taking only the white people in the  original data set and then randomly  reassigning race labels so at this point  race is totally uncorrelated with  outcome our ground truth is totally  totally balanced that's one hypothetical  world the other hypothetical world is  where we use the original imbalance data  set so in the raw data white people were  dramatically more likely to sign up for  the services of this agency and so we  use that as another hypothetical world  where we're saying that's ground truth  and ground truth is imbalanced so that's  our foundation these two different  worlds next we're going to introduce  known amounts of these different types  of bias  so remember subtlety number two was your  data is not the truth  it's a biased representation of the  truth and there's two different ways it  can be biased or there's lots of ways  but in this case we're talking about two  different ways it can be biased one of  these is with sample bias so in these  two different worlds we then sampled  from the sub population differentially  based on score thresholds and race  assignments so to introduce sample bias  we preferentially sampled white people  with high scores and avoided our sampled  at a lower rate white people with low  scores and then sampled black people  uniformly so this is injecting some  known sample bias into these datasets  that we're gonna use to train some  models in a similar fashion we injected  some known label bias so remember our  original original labels are  probabilities that you'll sign up for  this agency service we converted those  probabilities into binary labels but  used a different threshold for white  people and for black people so we used a  low threshold for white people and a  high threshold for black people so using  this we can start with like different  ground true so that we know different  hypothetical worlds inject these  different types of bias train a model on  the resulting data set and then apply  these different fairness metrics to the  outcome of to the predictions of those  models to see how well these different  metrics do in these different  hypothetical worlds with different types  of bias present in our training data so  that's that's the set up of the  experiment the two metrics that I'm  going to talk about here are disparate  impact and equal opportunities those are  the ones I mentioned previously that are  kind of generally popular in the  literature and you hear people talk  about in the actual paper we have a  paper written up and post on the archive  you address a couple other metrics so if  you're interested I encourage you to go  look up the paper on the archive but  we're applying these two metrics to  those two hypothetical worlds and for  different situations where we either  have the no no bias  we've introduced different types of bias  and this is what the results look like  in the hypothetical world where ground  truth is balanced so on the Left we have  results for disparate impact this is the  ratio based comparison on the right we  have the results for equal opportunity  which is the difference based comparison  for difference for disparate impact  because it's a ratio values of 1  indicate no measured unfairness and for  equal opportunity values of zero because  it's a difference indicate no measured  unfairness so what you see here is in  this hypothetical world where ground  truth is balanced between the classes  these metrics work pretty well  like they don't detect any bias they  don't measure bias in the case where we  have not artificially introduced by us  but we when we artificially introduce  sample bias or label bias they start  detecting more bias and then when we  have both types of artificially injected  bias in our training data set the the  fairness metrics detect that is being  even worse than either independently so  so far so good when your ground truth is  balanced these metrics are performing  fairly well unfortunately the picture is  dramatically less rosy if you're in a  hypothetical world where ground truth is  not balanced between your classes so  this is the the same same measurement  disparate impact over here on the left  equal opportunity a value of 1 means no  measured unfairness over here a value of  0 means no measured unfairness over here  and we can see that in the unbalanced  ground truth case both of these metrics  are measuring dramatic unfairness even  when we haven't injected bias into our  data and even worse it's even worse when  you look at label bias in particular  because if you compare the case where we  haven't injected any bias into our  training data to the case where we've  actually deliberately injected label  bias both of these metrics give you more  or less the same result and if you think  about it that kind of makes sense  because label bias is about labels being  assigned differently between groups  but if ground truth is different  legitimately between groups you're  already going to have differences in  labels assigned between groups so it's  especially hard to detect this kind of  bias so oh and here's a link to the  white paper here I describe these two  metrics therefore more in the paper and  also a slightly more in-depth treatment  of kind of the reasoning appetite behind  the experimental setup and this framing  so if you're interested I encourage you  to go check that out so that's kind of  the story so what should we do about it  well there's not really a  one-size-fits-all solution other than  think hard about your inputs and outputs  think hard about what's going into your  model think hard about the process that  generated your data think hard about the  kind of the ground truth that you're  trying to model in the first place and  how well your your label your dependent  variable in your training data  approximates the thing that you actually  care about modeling think about what's  going into your model and think about  what's coming out of your model think  about where your predictions are going  what kind of effects what kinds of  consequences stem from the decisions  made by this model that you have these  metrics that we talked about today can  help along with other metrics there's  there's kind of quite a variety of  different metrics people have proposed  for measuring fairness but you have to  use them carefully you have to think  about these questions these subtleties  of what this fair actually mean in the  context of the model that I'm building  right here if you do have good answers  to those questions there are also  methods in the literature that will help  you train models that automatically  respect some of these metrics so you can  kind of include them in your objective  function in some sense but again you  really have to know you really have to  be certain that one of these metrics  matches what fairness really means in  the context of your problem and what  you're talking about  use a diverse team to create these  models and think about these questions  throughout this talk I've tried to  emphasize that it's really a question of  human judgment but if  everyone on your team thinks about  things in exactly the same way then  you're not going to have good coverage  on the kinds of issues the kinds of  things that you need to be thinking  about when you're assessing these  ethical questions of fairness so you  know have a diverse team when you're  doing this really human work of thinking  about what fairness means in the context  of your model and then finally just to  repeat like think about your data  think about your predictions think about  the effects that everything has I'll  leave you with this quote from Cathy  O'Neill's book weapons of mass  destruction about how this is really a  human moral question that your data is  not the truth and even if it were the  truth is it the kind of truth that you  want to see in the world like that's  that's a really human question and we  can't math our way out of having to use  human judgment to answer it thank you  [Applause]  thank you very much really important and  well delivered talk um am i 